COURSES = [
{'slug': 'ai-for-beginners-course', 'title': 'Free AI Course for Beginners &amp; Over-60s | 365 Techies', 'metaDesc': 'Learn what AI chatbots like ChatGPT really are, what they do brilliantly, and what never to tell them. Free, senior-paced lessons with a printable certificate.', 'h1': 'AI, <em class="grad grad--green">without the mystery</em>', 'heroLede': 'A free, friendly course from the family-run firm that has been untangling technology in Bournemouth since 1995. Seven short lessons explain what AI chatbots really are, what they do brilliantly and what to keep to yourself &mdash; all at your own pace, with a printable certificate at the end. No jargon, nothing to install, nothing to pay.', 'icon': '&#129302;', 'courseTitle': 'AI, Without the Mystery', 'tagline': 'Free &middot; 7 short lessons &middot; about 40 minutes', 'introBody': '<p>Welcome &mdash; and well done for being curious. AI is the biggest change to computers since the internet arrived, yet most explanations of it seem designed to confuse. This course is different. It was written for people who would rather have things explained properly, at a comfortable pace, with no jargon and no showing off.</p><p>Work through the lessons in order, or dip in wherever you like. After each one there is a gentle &ldquo;what would you do?&rdquo; question &mdash; not a test, just a chance to feel the ideas click into place. There is no timer and no pass mark, and your certificate is waiting at the end.</p>', 'modules': [{'ico': '&#128173;', 'tag': 'Lesson 1 of 7', 'title': 'What AI chat actually is', 'body': '<p>You have probably heard the letters AI everywhere lately &mdash; on the news, from the grandchildren, perhaps at the surgery. AI stands for artificial intelligence, and the kind everyone is talking about is the chat kind: a website where you type a question in ordinary English and a helpful answer comes back, rather like a text conversation.</p><p>Here is the secret that takes away the mystery. An AI chatbot is not a mind, and it is not magic. It is a computer program that has read an enormous amount of writing &mdash; books, articles, websites &mdash; and become remarkably good at predicting which words usually come next. Think of it as a very well-read helper with an extraordinary memory for how sentences go.</p><p>That is why it can write a lovely letter in seconds &mdash; and also why it sometimes gets facts wrong, which we will come to in Lesson 4. It does not know things the way you do; it is simply very good at putting words together. Once you see it that way, it stops being frightening and starts being useful.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'A neighbour tells you AI chatbots are thinking machines that know everything. What is closest to the truth?', 'opts': [{'t': 'They are clever programs that predict words &mdash; very well-read, but not minds, and not always right', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Spot on. Keep that picture in your head and the rest of this course will feel like common sense.'}, {'t': 'They are thinking machines that know everything and never make mistakes', 'ok': False, 'why': 'An easy impression to get &mdash; they certainly sound sure of themselves. But they are word-predictors, not minds, and they do make mistakes. Lesson 4 will show you exactly how to handle that.'}, {'t': 'They are a passing fad that will be gone by Christmas', 'ok': False, 'why': 'AI helpers look like they are here to stay &mdash; which is exactly why a gentle course like this is worth your time. No rush, though: they will still be there whenever you are ready.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128075;', 'tag': 'Lesson 2 of 7', 'title': 'Meeting an AI helper safely', 'body': '<p>Here is some lovely news: you do not need to buy anything or install anything. The three best-known AI helpers all have free versions you can use in your web browser, at the time of writing:</p><ul><li><strong>ChatGPT</strong>, made by OpenAI &mdash; at <strong>chatgpt.com</strong></li><li><strong>Microsoft Copilot</strong> &mdash; at <strong>copilot.microsoft.com</strong>, and also built into recent versions of Windows</li><li><strong>Google Gemini</strong> &mdash; at <strong>gemini.google.com</strong></li></ul><p>They all work in much the same way: a box where you type your question, a button to send it (usually an arrow symbol), and the reply appears underneath. Some ask you to sign in with an email address for the free version. The exact screens vary by app and change over time, so take it slowly and read what is in front of you &mdash; there is never any rush.</p><p>Our advice: pick <em>one</em> and get comfortable with it. There is no wrong choice &mdash; they are more alike than different &mdash; and you cannot break anything by asking a question. (They all work on Apple devices too, but our examples stick to Windows and Android.)</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'You would like to try an AI helper for the very first time. What is the gentlest way to start?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Buy a subscription first, because the free versions must be a trick', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A sensible instinct to be wary &mdash; but in this case the big three genuinely offer free versions at the time of writing. Start free; you may never need anything more.'}, {'t': 'Open one of the free ones &mdash; such as copilot.microsoft.com &mdash; in your web browser and type a simple question', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Perfect. Nothing to install, nothing to pay, and nothing can go wrong from asking a question. That is genuinely all there is to it.'}, {'t': 'Download an app from a link in an email advert', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Good caution needed here: links in adverts and unexpected emails are how dodgy apps spread. If you ever want an app on your phone, get it from the official Google Play Store and check the reviews first &mdash; but for these helpers, the website version is all you need.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#127775;', 'tag': 'Lesson 3 of 7', 'title': 'What it is brilliant at', 'body': '<p>This is the joyful part. An AI helper is like having a patient, well-read friend on call, and it shines at everyday jobs:</p><ul><li><strong>Drafting letters</strong> &mdash; a complaint about a late delivery, a note to the council, a thank-you that strikes just the right tone</li><li><strong>Explaining jargon</strong> &mdash; paste in a baffling sentence from a bill or a letter and ask, &ldquo;What does this mean in plain English?&rdquo;</li><li><strong>Recipes from leftovers</strong> &mdash; tell it what is in the fridge and ask what you could make tonight</li><li><strong>Hobby research</strong> &mdash; family history, garden birds, crossword clues, the history of your street</li><li><strong>Poems and verses</strong> &mdash; a funny rhyme for a birthday card, written about the actual person</li></ul><p>The trick that makes it all work: you can always ask again. Say &ldquo;shorter, please&rdquo;, &ldquo;make it friendlier&rdquo; or &ldquo;explain that more simply&rdquo; and it will redo the whole thing without a flicker of impatience. It never gets tired, and it never thinks a question is silly.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'You want to complain politely to a company about a late delivery, but the words just will not come. What could you try?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Tell the chatbot what happened and ask for a polite draft, then read it, tweak it and send it yourself', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly right. It does the wording; you stay in charge. Most people find the draft needs only a tweak or two &mdash; and asking for a rewrite costs nothing.'}, {'t': 'Ask the chatbot to sort the whole thing out, and assume the job is done', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A very understandable hope! But the chatbot can only put words on your screen &mdash; it cannot post letters or ring the company. Think of it as a writing partner: it drafts, you check and send.'}, {'t': 'Struggle on alone &mdash; a machine could never help with something personal like this', 'ok': False, 'why': 'You might be pleasantly surprised. Finding the right words is one of the things AI helpers do best. You supply the facts and the feelings; it supplies the sentences; the final say is always yours.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#129300;', 'tag': 'Lesson 4 of 7', 'title': 'Its big weakness: confidently wrong', 'body': '<p>Now for the most important lesson in the course. AI chatbots sometimes get things wrong &mdash; and here is the tricky part: they get things wrong <em>confidently</em>. A wrong answer arrives in exactly the same fluent, friendly tone as a right one. Computer people call these mistakes &ldquo;hallucinations&rdquo;, which is just a fancy word for the chatbot making something up without realising it has.</p><p>Why does it happen? Remember Lesson 1: the chatbot predicts words. Usually that produces the truth, because it has read so much. But sometimes it produces something that merely <em>sounds</em> true &mdash; a made-up date, a wrong dose, a phone number that does not exist.</p><p>So here is the golden rule of the whole course: <strong>double-check any fact that matters before you act on it.</strong> For anything medical, legal or to do with money, treat the chatbot as a starting point only &mdash; then check with your pharmacist, your GP, your bank or another source you trust. A birthday poem needs no checking. A pill always does.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">You ask about some berries growing in your garden. The chatbot replies:</p><p>&ldquo;Good news &mdash; those berries are perfectly safe to eat.&rdquo;</p></div>', 'q': 'It sounds completely certain. What do you do?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Eat them &mdash; the chatbot sounded very sure of itself', 'ok': False, 'why': 'That confident tone is exactly the trap. Chatbots sound just as sure when they are wrong as when they are right. Anything that could affect your health deserves a proper human check first.'}, {'t': 'Ask the same chatbot again &mdash; if it says yes twice, it must be right', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A clever thought, but it can happily repeat the same mistake twice. Confidence is not the same as accuracy &mdash; for anything that matters, check with a source outside the chatbot.'}, {'t': 'Treat it as a guess, not gospel &mdash; check with a trusted source such as a gardening book, a garden centre or an expert before anyone eats a thing', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly right. When the stakes are real &mdash; food, medicines, money, the law &mdash; a chatbot&rsquo;s answer is a starting point, never the final word. You have just learned the most valuable habit in this course.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128274;', 'tag': 'Lesson 5 of 7', 'title': 'What never to tell it', 'body': '<p>Chatting with an AI helper can feel private, like writing in a diary. It is not. Your conversations travel over the internet to the company&rsquo;s computers, and may be stored or used to improve the service. Nothing sinister &mdash; but it leads to one simple rule:</p><p><strong>Never type anything into a chatbot that you would not write on a postcard.</strong> That means:</p><ul><li>No passwords or PINs &mdash; ever, for anything</li><li>No card numbers or bank details</li><li>No full set of personal details together &mdash; full name plus address plus date of birth</li><li>Nothing deeply private that you would hate a stranger to read</li></ul><p>The good news: you almost never need any of that. Asking for help with a letter to your bank? Write &ldquo;[account number]&rdquo; as a placeholder and fill in the real one by hand afterwards. And remember: no genuine company will ever ask you to type a password or a one-time code into a chat &mdash; a request like that is always a red flag.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">You are asking a chatbot for help:</p><p>&ldquo;Please write a firm but polite letter to my bank about a payment that went missing on the 3rd of June&hellip;&rdquo;</p></div>', 'q': 'Should you include your account number and password so the letter is complete?', 'opts': [{'t': 'No &mdash; just tell it the story, write [account number] as a placeholder, and add the real details by hand afterwards', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Perfect. The chatbot never needs your real details to write an excellent letter &mdash; placeholders do the job, and your private information stays exactly where it belongs: with you.'}, {'t': 'Yes &mdash; the more detail it has, the better the letter will be', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A natural thought, but this is the one to avoid. A chatbot is not a private diary &mdash; and no letter ever needs your password anyway. Remember the postcard rule: if you would not write it on a postcard, do not type it into a chat.'}, {'t': 'Include the account number but keep the password back', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Halfway there &mdash; good instinct on the password! But bank details belong on the postcard-rule list too. A placeholder like [account number] gets you a perfect letter with nothing private shared at all.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128737;&#65039;', 'tag': 'Lesson 6 of 7', 'title': 'AI scams to watch for', 'body': '<p>AI has handed scammers some new tools, and forewarned is forearmed. The big one to know about is <strong>voice cloning</strong>: from a short clip of someone speaking &mdash; a video shared online is enough &mdash; software can fake a phone call that sounds exactly like a loved one. Combine that with the well-known &ldquo;Hi Mum&rdquo; trick on WhatsApp, where a message from a &ldquo;child&rdquo; on a &ldquo;new number&rdquo; asks for money urgently, and you can see the danger. (One more WhatsApp rule worth knowing: never read out or pass on a six-digit code that arrives by text message &mdash; that code is the key to your account, and tricking people into sharing it is how accounts get stolen.)</p><p>The defence is wonderfully old-fashioned: <strong>agree a family codeword</strong> &mdash; a word only your family knows. Any emergency call asking for money? Ask for the codeword. And whatever the voice says, hang up and ring the person back on the number you already have for them. Real family will not mind one bit.</p><p>AI can also produce fake photos and videos that look startlingly real. So the golden rule of scam safety applies double now: anything urgent, emotional and about money deserves a slow, calm double-check. And if the worst happens, act quickly and without a shred of embarrassment: call your bank straight away &mdash; dialling <strong>159</strong> connects you safely to most UK banks &mdash; forward scam texts free to <strong>7726</strong>, forward scam emails to <strong>report@phishing.gov.uk</strong>, and report it to <strong>Action Fraud</strong> on <strong>0300 123 2040</strong> (in Scotland, tell Police Scotland on 101). And remember: banks will never ask you to move money to a &ldquo;safe account&rdquo; &mdash; anyone who does is a scammer.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">Your phone rings. A voice that sounds exactly like your grandson says:</p><p>&ldquo;Gran, it&rsquo;s me &mdash; I&rsquo;m in trouble and my phone&rsquo;s broken. I need &pound;300 today. Please don&rsquo;t tell Mum.&rdquo;</p></div>', 'q': 'It truly sounds like him. What do you do?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Send the money at once &mdash; you would never forgive yourself if it were real', 'ok': False, 'why': 'That feeling is precisely what the scam runs on &mdash; urgency and love. But a two-minute call-back on a trusted number costs a real grandson nothing and saves you from a cruel trick. Slow down first, always.'}, {'t': 'Stay calm, ask for the family codeword, then hang up and ring him back on the number you already have', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly right. The codeword and the call-back defeat even a perfect voice clone. If he is really in trouble, he will still be in trouble in two minutes &mdash; and now you will know it is truly him.'}, {'t': 'Keep him talking and ask questions only he would know the answers to', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A clever instinct &mdash; but scammers do their homework, and family details often sit on social media. The test that never fails is simpler: hang up and ring back on the number you already trust.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#9749;', 'tag': 'Lesson 7 of 7', 'title': 'Try it today: your first questions', 'body': '<p>The best way to take the mystery out of AI is simply to have a go. Put the kettle on, open ChatGPT, Copilot or Gemini, and try any of these:</p><ul><li>&ldquo;Explain what a QR code is, in plain English, for someone in their seventies.&rdquo;</li><li>&ldquo;I have eggs, cheese, a leek and some tired-looking potatoes. What could I cook tonight?&rdquo;</li><li>&ldquo;Write a short, funny poem for my sister&rsquo;s 70th birthday. She loves gardening and strong tea.&rdquo;</li><li>&ldquo;Suggest some gentle exercises I can do sitting down.&rdquo; (Anything health-related, run past your GP too &mdash; Lesson 4!)</li><li>&ldquo;Tell me something interesting about the history of Bournemouth.&rdquo;</li></ul><p>Two habits to keep from day one. First: if the answer misses the mark, just ask again in different words &mdash; it never gets impatient, and neither should you. Second: enjoy the help, but double-check anything that matters. Do that, and you are using AI more wisely than most people half your age.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'Which of these makes the best first question for an AI helper?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Whether you should sell your house &mdash; including your address and savings details so it can advise properly', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Two lessons rolled into one here! Big money decisions belong with qualified humans you trust, and personal details never belong in a chat. By all means ask it to explain house-selling jargon &mdash; just keep the private details and the final decision with people.'}, {'t': 'Nothing at all, in case you break something', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Here is the freeing truth: you cannot break anything by asking a question. The worst that can happen is a dull answer &mdash; in which case you simply ask again. Go on, have a go.'}, {'t': 'Something you have always wondered about, asked in your own words &mdash; and &ldquo;explain it simply&rdquo; if the answer is too clever', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Lovely choice. Curiosity is the perfect starting point, and asking for a simpler answer is not cheating &mdash; it is exactly how these helpers are meant to be used.'}]}}], 'doneBlurb': '<p>Congratulations &mdash; you have finished the course, and you now understand AI better than a great many people who use it every day. If you keep just one habit, make it this: <strong>enjoy the help, but double-check any fact that matters before you act on it</strong> &mdash; and never share passwords, card details or full personal details with a chatbot.</p><p>And remember, you are never on your own with technology. Real, friendly help is one phone call away on <strong>01202 775566</strong>, and we run relaxed in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth. Do come and say hello.</p>', 'certCourseName': 'AI, Without the Mystery', 'certSubline': 'Understanding AI helpers: what they are, what they do well, what to keep private, and how to spot AI scams', 'checklist': ['Remember what AI chat is: a well-read helper that predicts words - clever, but not a mind and not always right.', 'Try one free helper in your web browser: chatgpt.com, copilot.microsoft.com or gemini.google.com. Nothing to install.', 'Use it for the good stuff: drafting letters, explaining jargon, recipes from leftovers, hobby research, birthday poems.', 'Double-check any fact that matters before acting on it - especially health, legal and money questions.', 'Never type passwords, PINs, card numbers or bank details into a chatbot.', 'Keep full personal details out of chats - use placeholders like [account number] in draft letters.', 'Agree a family codeword to defeat AI voice-cloning calls asking for money.', 'For any urgent call about money: hang up and ring the person back on a number you already trust.', 'If a scam strikes: dial 159 to reach your bank, forward scam texts to 7726 and scam emails to report@phishing.gov.uk, and tell Action Fraud on 0300 123 2040.', 'Stuck or unsure? Call 365 Techies on 01202 775566 - friendly local help since 1995.'], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Is this course really free?', 'a': '<p>Completely free, with no catch and no card details asked for. We are a family-run IT firm that has looked after Bournemouth homes and businesses since 1995, and this course is our way of helping local people feel confident with new technology.</p>'}, {'q': 'Do I need to install anything or create an account?', 'a': '<p>Nothing at all for this course &mdash; it runs right here in your web browser. The AI helpers themselves (ChatGPT, Microsoft Copilot, Google Gemini) may ask you to sign in with an email address for their free versions, but that is entirely up to you and can wait until you feel ready.</p>'}, {'q': 'Is the certificate a qualification?', 'a': '<p>It is a 365 Techies certificate of completion &mdash; a lovely thing to print, keep or show the grandchildren, but not an accredited qualification. The real prize is the confidence and the safe habits you take away with you.</p>'}, {'q': 'I have never used AI before. Is this too advanced for me?', 'a': '<p>Quite the opposite &mdash; it was written especially for complete beginners. Every idea is explained in plain English, every lesson is short, and there is no timer, no pass mark and no way to fail. You set the pace, and you can revisit any lesson as often as you like.</p>'}, {'q': 'Is AI actually safe to use?', 'a': '<p>Yes &mdash; used with the habits in this course. Ask it anything you like, but double-check facts that matter, keep passwords and bank details out of your chats, and never let it make medical, legal or money decisions on its own. Follow those rules and it is a genuinely helpful companion.</p>'}, {'q': 'Can I get help in person?', 'a': '<p>Absolutely. We run relaxed, friendly in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth &mdash; no question too small. Call us on 01202 775566 and we will find a time that suits you.</p>'}], 'crumbName': 'AI, Without the Mystery'},
{'slug': 'whatsapp-course-for-beginners', 'title': 'Free WhatsApp Course for Beginners &amp; Over 60s | 365 Techies', 'metaDesc': 'A free, friendly WhatsApp course for older beginners. Learn messages, photos, voice notes, video calls and staying safe, at your own pace, with a certificate.', 'h1': 'WhatsApp, <em class="grad grad--green">step by step</em>', 'heroLede': 'The messages, the photos, the video calls &mdash; all easier than you think, and all free over Wi-Fi. This free course walks you through WhatsApp one gentle lesson at a time, entirely at your own pace, with no jargon and nothing to break. Finish the lessons and print a certificate with your name on it.', 'icon': '&#128172;', 'courseTitle': 'WhatsApp, Step by Step', 'tagline': 'Free · 7 short lessons · about 35 minutes', 'introBody': '<p>Welcome &mdash; and well done for being here. WhatsApp is how millions of families share their days: quick messages, photos of the grandchildren, video calls that make the miles disappear. If you&rsquo;ve ever felt left out of the family group chat, this course is for you.</p><p>There&rsquo;s no rush and no test you can fail. Each lesson is short, written in plain English, and ends with one gentle &ldquo;what would you do?&rdquo; question. Have your phone next to you if you can &mdash; and remember, you cannot break anything by trying.</p>', 'modules': [{'ico': '&#128075;', 'tag': 'Lesson 1 of 7', 'title': 'What WhatsApp is (and why your family loves it)', 'body': '<p>WhatsApp is a free app for sending messages and photos, and for making voice and video calls over the internet. On your phone it&rsquo;s the green icon with a white telephone inside a speech bubble.</p><p>Because it works over your home Wi-Fi, there&rsquo;s nothing extra to pay &mdash; a message to your neighbour costs the same as a video call to a grandchild in Australia: nothing. That&rsquo;s why families everywhere use it for sharing photos, keeping group chats going and seeing each other&rsquo;s faces.</p><p>It&rsquo;s private, too. WhatsApp messages and calls are end-to-end encrypted, which simply means they&rsquo;re scrambled so that only you and the person you&rsquo;re talking to can read or hear them.</p><p>This course describes WhatsApp as it appears on Android phones, the kind most of our customers have. WhatsApp works on Apple iPhones too &mdash; it&rsquo;s the same app and looks very similar, so everything here still applies.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'Your son lives in Australia and suggests a WhatsApp video call. What will it cost you?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Nothing extra, as long as I&rsquo;m connected to my home Wi-Fi', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly right. WhatsApp calls travel over the internet, so a call to Australia costs no more than one to next door &mdash; nothing at all on Wi-Fi.'}, {'t': 'Expensive international call rates, so best keep it short', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Good news &mdash; that&rsquo;s the old way! WhatsApp calls go over the internet, so on your home Wi-Fi they cost nothing, however far away family are and however long you chat.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128172;', 'tag': 'Lesson 2 of 7', 'title': 'Sending your first message', 'body': '<p>Open WhatsApp by tapping the green icon. You&rsquo;ll see a list of your chats &mdash; conversations you&rsquo;ve had, newest at the top. If the list is empty, that&rsquo;s fine: yours is a fresh start.</p><p>To start a new chat, look for the new-chat button &mdash; usually a round green button with a speech bubble or plus sign on it (its exact look varies a little by phone maker and app version). Tap it, then choose the person you want from your contact list.</p><p>Tap the long typing box at the bottom, type your message, and when you&rsquo;re happy, tap the send button &mdash; the little arrow beside the box. Here&rsquo;s the most reassuring part: <strong>nothing is sent until you tap that arrow</strong>. You can take five seconds or five minutes. WhatsApp will wait.</p><p>After sending, you&rsquo;ll see small ticks next to your message: one tick means it has been sent, two ticks mean it has arrived on their phone.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'You&rsquo;ve typed a message to your friend but haven&rsquo;t tapped the arrow yet. Has it been sent?', 'opts': [{'t': 'No &mdash; nothing goes anywhere until I tap the send arrow', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Spot on. You can pause, reread and change your mind as often as you like. WhatsApp only sends when you tap the arrow &mdash; you are always in charge.'}, {'t': 'Yes &mdash; it sends as I type, so I need to be quick', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Happily not! Nothing is sent until you tap the arrow, so there&rsquo;s no need to rush. Take all the time you like &mdash; the message simply sits there waiting for you.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128247;', 'tag': 'Lesson 3 of 7', 'title': 'Photos and voice notes (a gift for tired fingers)', 'body': '<p>To send a photo, open the chat and look next to the typing box for a camera or paperclip icon. The camera takes a new photo on the spot; the paperclip (on some phones a plus sign) lets you choose one already saved on your phone. Pick your photo, add a caption if you like, and tap send.</p><p>Now for many people&rsquo;s favourite discovery: <strong>voice notes</strong>. Instead of typing, press and hold the microphone icon beside the typing box, speak your message, then let go to send it. Your voice arrives just as you said it &mdash; warm, quick, and no fiddly typing. For anyone who finds small keyboards a chore, this is genuinely liberating.</p><p>If a recording goes wrong halfway through, don&rsquo;t worry &mdash; slide your finger towards the bin to cancel it and start again. Nobody ever hears the false starts.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'Typing feels slow and fiddly today. What&rsquo;s the easiest way to send a longer message?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Hold down the microphone button and simply speak my message', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Yes! Voice notes are made for exactly this. Speak naturally, let go, and it&rsquo;s sent &mdash; many people try one and never look back.'}, {'t': 'Struggle on with typing &mdash; there&rsquo;s no other way', 'ok': False, 'why': 'There&rsquo;s a much easier way! Press and hold the microphone icon beside the typing box and just talk. Voice notes are one of WhatsApp&rsquo;s kindest features.'}, {'t': 'Wait for a grandchild to visit and type it for me', 'ok': False, 'why': 'They&rsquo;d be happy to help &mdash; but you don&rsquo;t need to wait for anyone. Hold the microphone button and speak; your message sends the moment you let go.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128249;', 'tag': 'Lesson 4 of 7', 'title': 'Video calling the family', 'body': '<p>Seeing faces is what WhatsApp does best. Open the chat with the person you want, then tap the <strong>video camera icon</strong> near the top of the screen. That&rsquo;s it &mdash; the call starts ringing on their phone.</p><p>Two small tricks make a big difference. First, light: sit facing a window or lamp, not with it behind you, so your face is bright rather than in shadow. Second, steadiness: prop the phone against something &mdash; a pile of books, a mug, a little stand &mdash; rather than holding it at arm&rsquo;s length. Your arm (and the picture) will thank you.</p><p>When someone video calls you, your phone rings and shows an answer button &mdash; tap (or on some phones swipe) the green accept button and say hello. On Wi-Fi the whole thing is free, however long you natter, and nobody minds what the kitchen looks like behind you.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'On a video call, your daughter says your face is just a dark shadow. What&rsquo;s the fix?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Turn so the window or lamp is in front of me, lighting my face', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Perfect. Light in front, not behind &mdash; it&rsquo;s the one tip that makes everyone instantly clearer. Add a propped-up phone and you&rsquo;ll look like a natural.'}, {'t': 'Sit with my back to the bright window', 'ok': False, 'why': 'So close &mdash; it&rsquo;s the other way round! With the window behind you, the camera sees only a silhouette. Face the light and your family will see you beautifully.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128106;', 'tag': 'Lesson 5 of 7', 'title': 'Groups, without the overwhelm', 'body': '<p>A group is one shared chat where everyone in it &mdash; the whole family, the book club, the bowls team &mdash; sees every message. It&rsquo;s wonderful for sharing news and photos with everybody at once.</p><p>But a lively group can ping all day, and that can feel like a lot. Here&rsquo;s the secret nobody tells beginners: <strong>you can mute a group, and it is not rude</strong>. Open the group and find the Mute option &mdash; usually by tapping the group&rsquo;s name at the top of the chat (menus vary a little by phone and app version) &mdash; then choose how long you&rsquo;d like the quiet to last, from a few hours right up to always. The messages still arrive; they just arrive quietly, ready for when you fancy a browse with a cup of tea.</p><p>And a gentle rule for your own peace of mind: you don&rsquo;t have to reply to everything. Nobody expects it &mdash; not even the person who sends forty photos of their dahlias.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'The family group pings all day and it&rsquo;s wearing you out. What&rsquo;s the kind, sensible fix?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Mute the group &mdash; messages still arrive, just quietly, for me to read when it suits', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly. Muting is the polite volume knob of WhatsApp. You stay in the group and miss nothing &mdash; you simply read on your own schedule.'}, {'t': 'Leave the group &mdash; it&rsquo;s the only way to get some peace', 'ok': False, 'why': 'You&rsquo;d miss all the photos! There&rsquo;s a gentler option: mute the group. Everything still arrives, silently, and nobody is any the wiser.'}, {'t': 'Reply to every single message so nobody is offended', 'ok': False, 'why': 'That would exhaust anyone! Group chats aren&rsquo;t like conversation &mdash; dipping in when you feel like it is completely normal, and nobody keeps score.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128737;&#65039;', 'tag': 'Lesson 6 of 7', 'title': 'Staying safe on WhatsApp', 'body': '<p>WhatsApp itself is safe &mdash; but scammers use it, just as they use the phone and the post. The one to know is the <strong>&ldquo;Hi Mum&rdquo; scam</strong> (or &ldquo;Hi Dad&rdquo;). A message arrives from an unknown number: &ldquo;Hi Mum, I&rsquo;ve broken my phone &mdash; this is my new number&rdquo;. Soon after comes an urgent request for money. It feels real because it uses your love against you.</p><p>The golden rule: <strong>never send money because of a message alone</strong>. Ring your child or grandchild on the number you&rsquo;ve always had for them &mdash; the old number &mdash; or ring another family member. A real emergency survives a two-minute phone call; a scam doesn&rsquo;t. Many families also agree a private codeword only they know, which beats even the AI voice-cloning tricks scammers now use to fake family emergency calls.</p><p>Two more habits. If a stranger&rsquo;s message looks fishy, open the chat and use <strong>Report</strong> and <strong>Block</strong> &mdash; WhatsApp offers both for any contact. And never share the <strong>6-digit code</strong> WhatsApp texts you &mdash; not with anyone, even someone claiming to be family or WhatsApp itself. That code is the key to your account, and handing it over is exactly how accounts get stolen.</p><p>And if a scammer ever does catch you out, you&rsquo;re in good company and it is never your fault &mdash; these tricks fool sharp, kind people every day. Ring your bank straight away (dialling <strong>159</strong> connects you securely to most UK banks), then report it to <strong>Action Fraud on 0300 123 2040</strong>. Acting quickly gives your bank the best chance of stopping the money.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">A message arrives from a number you don&rsquo;t recognise:</p><p>&ldquo;Hi Mum, it&rsquo;s me &mdash; dropped my phone down the loo so this is my new number! Bit of a nightmare&hellip; my card&rsquo;s blocked and a bill is due today. Could you send &pound;850? I&rsquo;ll pay you back Friday, promise xx&rdquo;</p></div>', 'q': 'What do you do first?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Ring my child on the number I&rsquo;ve always had for them (or ring another family member) before doing anything else', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Perfect &mdash; that phone call is the scam-killer. If it really is them, you&rsquo;ve lost two minutes. If it isn&rsquo;t, you&rsquo;ve just saved &pound;850. Then report and block the new number.'}, {'t': 'Send the money &mdash; it sounds urgent and they promised to pay it back', 'ok': False, 'why': 'This is the well-known &ldquo;Hi Mum&rdquo; scam, and it fools kind, loving people every single day &mdash; that&rsquo;s exactly who it&rsquo;s aimed at. Always ring the old number first; a real emergency can wait two minutes.'}, {'t': 'Reply to the new number and ask if it&rsquo;s really them', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A good instinct to check &mdash; but a scammer will simply answer &ldquo;yes, it&rsquo;s me!&rdquo;. The only check that works is ringing the number you&rsquo;ve always had for them, or another family member.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#129529;', 'tag': 'Lesson 7 of 7', 'title': 'A little housekeeping', 'body': '<p>Photos and videos are the joy of WhatsApp &mdash; and, over time, the thing that fills a phone up. If your phone grumbles about storage, WhatsApp&rsquo;s settings include storage tools that show which chats take up the most space and let you clear out old photos and videos you no longer need. Deleting them from your phone doesn&rsquo;t remove them from the sender&rsquo;s phone, so nothing precious is lost to the family.</p><p>It&rsquo;s also worth a quick privacy check. In WhatsApp&rsquo;s <strong>Settings</strong>, under <strong>Privacy</strong>, you can choose who sees your profile photo &mdash; the choices include everyone, just your contacts, or nobody at all. &ldquo;My contacts&rdquo; is a comfortable choice for most people &mdash; family see your smiling face, strangers don&rsquo;t.</p><p>Finally, when your phone offers an update &mdash; for WhatsApp through the Google Play Store, or for Android itself &mdash; say yes. Updates are free, and they patch the security holes scammers hunt for. An up-to-date phone is a safer phone.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'You&rsquo;d rather strangers couldn&rsquo;t see your profile photo. Is that possible?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Yes &mdash; in WhatsApp&rsquo;s privacy settings I can limit it to my contacts, or hide it completely', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Quite right. It&rsquo;s a one-minute change under Privacy in WhatsApp&rsquo;s settings, and then only the people you choose see your photo.'}, {'t': 'No &mdash; profile photos are always public', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Good news: you have full control. WhatsApp&rsquo;s privacy settings let you show your photo to everyone, just your contacts, or nobody at all.'}]}}], 'doneBlurb': '<p>Well done &mdash; you&rsquo;ve finished the whole course! You can now send messages, share photos, speak a voice note, video call the family, tame a noisy group and spot the scams. That&rsquo;s a proper set of skills, learned entirely at your own pace.</p><p>If you keep just one habit, keep this one: <strong>if any message asks for money or a code, stop and ring the person on the number you&rsquo;ve always had for them</strong>. And remember &mdash; help is only ever one phone call away on <strong>01202 775566</strong>, and we run friendly in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth.</p>', 'certCourseName': 'WhatsApp, Step by Step', 'certSubline': 'Messaging, photos, voice notes, video calls, groups and staying safe on WhatsApp', 'checklist': ['Look for the green icon with a white phone in a speech bubble.', 'Nothing sends until you tap the send arrow, so take your time.', 'Hold the microphone button to speak a voice note instead of typing.', 'Send photos with the camera or paperclip icon next to the typing box.', 'On video calls, face the light and prop your phone steady.', 'Mute noisy groups. It is not rude and you still get every message.', 'New number asking for money? Ring the old number first. Agree a family codeword too.', 'Never share your 6-digit WhatsApp code with anyone at all.', 'Sent money to a scammer? Ring your bank first (dial 159), then Action Fraud on 0300 123 2040.', 'Say yes to free updates. Stuck? Call 365 Techies on 01202 775566.'], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Is WhatsApp really free?', 'a': '<p>Yes. The app is free, and messages and calls cost nothing when you&rsquo;re on Wi-Fi &mdash; even to family abroad. Away from Wi-Fi it uses your mobile data allowance instead, so if you&rsquo;re mostly at home, it costs you nothing at all.</p>'}, {'q': 'Do I need a computer for this course?', 'a': '<p>No &mdash; just a comfy chair. The course reads nicely on a phone, tablet or computer screen, but WhatsApp itself lives on your phone, so it helps to have the phone beside you as you go through the lessons.</p>'}, {'q': 'Is the certificate a qualification?', 'a': '<p>It&rsquo;s a 365 Techies certificate of completion &mdash; a lovely marker of what you&rsquo;ve learned, with your name on it, ready to print. It isn&rsquo;t a formal qualification, but it&rsquo;s certainly something to be proud of and pop on the fridge.</p>'}, {'q': 'I don&rsquo;t have WhatsApp on my phone yet. What do I do?', 'a': '<p>On an Android phone, open the <strong>Google Play Store</strong>, search for WhatsApp and tap Install &mdash; it&rsquo;s free. You&rsquo;ll confirm your mobile number, and WhatsApp will text you a 6-digit code to type in (keep that code to yourself, always). If you&rsquo;d like a friendly hand setting it up, that&rsquo;s exactly what our Kinson lessons are for.</p>'}, {'q': 'What if I make a mistake and send something silly?', 'a': '<p>It happens to everyone, and it&rsquo;s never a disaster. If you spot it soon after sending, WhatsApp even lets you delete the message for everyone in the chat &mdash; and honestly, family are usually just delighted to hear from you. There&rsquo;s no mistake on WhatsApp that can&rsquo;t be fixed or laughed off.</p>'}, {'q': 'Can I get help in person?', 'a': '<p>Yes! We run relaxed, in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth, and we&rsquo;re always at the end of the phone on <strong>01202 775566</strong>. No question is too small &mdash; we&rsquo;ve been helping local people with their technology since 1995.</p>'}], 'crumbName': 'WhatsApp, Step by Step'},
{'slug': 'android-phone-course-for-beginners', 'title': 'Free Android Phone Course for Beginners | 365 Techies', 'metaDesc': 'A free Android phone course for older beginners &mdash; home screen, settings, apps, photos, updates and phone scams. Learn at your own pace, free certificate.', 'h1': 'Your Android phone, <em class="grad grad--green">tamed</em>', 'heroLede': 'A free, gentle course for anyone who wants to feel at home on their Android phone. Seven short lessons you can take entirely at your own pace &mdash; and a printable certificate with your name on it when you finish. No jargon, no rushing, no silly questions.', 'icon': '&#128241;', 'courseTitle': 'Your Android Phone, Tamed', 'tagline': 'Free · 7 short lessons · about 25 minutes', 'introBody': '<p>Welcome &mdash; and well done for being here. This course is for you if your Android phone sometimes feels like it belongs to someone cleverer. It doesn&rsquo;t. By the end of these seven short lessons, it will feel like yours.</p><p>Each lesson takes a few minutes, there&rsquo;s a gentle &ldquo;what would you do?&rdquo; question after each one, and you can stop for a cup of tea whenever you like &mdash; your place is saved. One thing before we start: <strong>you cannot break your phone by learning about it.</strong> Promise.</p>', 'modules': [{'ico': '&#127968;', 'tag': 'Lesson 1 of 7', 'title': 'The home screen, without fear', 'body': '<p>Here&rsquo;s the most important thing in this whole course: <strong>you cannot easily break your phone by tapping things</strong>. Apps are just doors &mdash; tapping one opens it, and you can always walk straight back out again.</p><p>Every Android phone has two faithful rescuers. The <strong>Home</strong> control &mdash; a button at the bottom, or a quick swipe up from the bottom edge, depending on your phone &mdash; takes you straight back to your familiar home screen from anywhere. The <strong>Back</strong> control &mdash; an arrow, or a swipe in from the side edge &mdash; takes you one step back. Makers such as Samsung, Google and Motorola arrange these slightly differently, but every Android phone has them.</p><p>So give yourself permission to explore. Tap an app, have a look around, then press Home. Nothing is lost, nothing is broken &mdash; you&rsquo;re just opening and closing doors.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">&#128241; On your phone</p>You tap something by accident and a screen you&rsquo;ve never seen before appears. Your heart sinks a little.</div>', 'q': 'What&rsquo;s the best thing to do?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Turn the phone off and leave it in a drawer', 'ok': False, 'why': 'You haven&rsquo;t broken anything &mdash; promise! Nothing that appears on screen has damaged your phone, and there&rsquo;s a much gentler way back.'}, {'t': 'Press Home (or swipe up) and start again from the home screen', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly. Home always brings you back to familiar ground, wherever you&rsquo;ve wandered. That&rsquo;s why you can explore without fear.'}, {'t': 'Tap buttons quickly until something looks familiar', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Very understandable, but hurried tapping can lead you further in. One calm press of Home is all it takes.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#9881;&#65039;', 'tag': 'Lesson 2 of 7', 'title': 'A few settings that change everything', 'body': '<p>A handful of settings make your phone dramatically nicer to use &mdash; and the best one takes two minutes: <strong>make the text bigger</strong>. Open the <strong>Settings</strong> app (its icon looks like a gear or cog), look for <strong>Display</strong>, then something like <strong>Font size</strong> or <strong>Text size</strong>, and slide it up until reading feels effortless. The exact wording and place vary a little by maker &mdash; and if you can&rsquo;t spot it, most phones have a search box at the top of Settings where you can type &ldquo;font&rdquo;. Either way it&rsquo;s in there, and it&rsquo;s the single best change most people ever make.</p><ul><li><strong>Volume</strong> &mdash; the buttons on the side of the phone turn the sound up and down</li><li><strong>Brightness</strong> &mdash; swipe down from the very top of the screen and you&rsquo;ll usually find a brightness slider. A brighter screen is easier to read, but uses more battery</li></ul><p>And a word about the internet. <strong>Wi-Fi</strong> is your home broadband &mdash; once your phone is connected, using it costs nothing extra. <strong>Mobile data</strong> is the internet over the phone network, which comes out of your monthly allowance &mdash; your phone switches to it automatically when you&rsquo;re out and about. At home, Wi-Fi is your friend.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'The writing on your phone always feels too small to read comfortably. What&rsquo;s the best fix?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Put up with it &mdash; that&rsquo;s just how phones are', 'ok': False, 'why': 'You really don&rsquo;t have to! Bigger text is built into every Android phone, it&rsquo;s free, and it takes about two minutes to switch on.'}, {'t': 'Open Settings and turn the text size up', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Spot on. It&rsquo;s usually under Display &mdash; the wording varies a little by phone, but it&rsquo;s always there. The single best change most people make.'}, {'t': 'Buy a new phone with a bigger screen', 'ok': False, 'why': 'No need to spend a penny &mdash; the phone you already own can show much bigger text. Try the free fix first.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128717;&#65039;', 'tag': 'Lesson 3 of 7', 'title': 'Apps and the Google Play Store, safely', 'body': '<p>Apps come from the <strong>Google Play Store</strong> &mdash; the official app shop that comes on Android phones (its icon is a colourful play-button triangle). Sticking to the Play Store is the golden rule: apps there are checked by Google, which makes it far safer than anything a website pop-up tries to offer you.</p><p>Before installing, take a quick look at two things:</p><ul><li><strong>Reviews and downloads</strong> &mdash; lots of downloads and plenty of happy reviews is a very good sign</li><li><strong>Permissions</strong> &mdash; the things an app asks to use. A crossword app asking for your contacts and messages is asking for far more than it needs, and that&rsquo;s a reason to pick a different one</li></ul><p>And here&rsquo;s the freeing part: <strong>uninstalling is easy and completely safe</strong>. On most phones you press and hold the app&rsquo;s icon and choose <strong>Uninstall</strong> (the exact steps vary a little by maker). Trying an app costs you nothing &mdash; if you don&rsquo;t like it, off it goes, and your phone is none the worse.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">&#128717;&#65039; In the Play Store</p>Two crossword apps look almost identical. One has thousands of cheerful reviews and asks for nothing unusual. The other has hardly any reviews and wants access to your contacts and messages.</div>', 'q': 'Which one would you install?', 'opts': [{'t': 'The well-reviewed one that asks for very little', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Perfect judgement. Lots of happy users and modest permissions are exactly the signs to look for.'}, {'t': 'The other one &mdash; maybe it needs my contacts for a good reason', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A crossword puzzle has no business reading your contacts or messages. When an app asks for far more than it needs, choose a different one &mdash; there&rsquo;s always another.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128247;', 'tag': 'Lesson 4 of 7', 'title': 'Photos: taking, finding, keeping', 'body': '<p>Your phone&rsquo;s camera is probably the best camera you&rsquo;ve ever owned. Open the <strong>Camera</strong> app, point, and tap the big round button &mdash; and on many phones a <strong>volume button</strong> takes the photo too, which is easier to press without wobbling.</p><p>Finding photos again is the bit that worries people, and it&rsquo;s easier than you&rsquo;d think. They all land in your photos app &mdash; usually <strong>Google Photos</strong> or an app called <strong>Gallery</strong>, depending on your phone &mdash; neatly sorted by date, newest first. Google Photos even lets you search by typing a word like &ldquo;dog&rdquo; or &ldquo;beach&rdquo;.</p><p>If your phone ever warns that <strong>storage is nearly full</strong>, don&rsquo;t start deleting in a hurry. Google Photos can back your pictures up to your Google account, and once they&rsquo;re safely backed up it offers a <strong>free up space</strong> option (at the time of writing) that removes only the copies already saved &mdash; your photos stay safe and viewable in the app.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'Your phone warns that storage is nearly full. What&rsquo;s the wisest move?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Quickly delete photos until the message goes away', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Deleting in a hurry is how treasured photos get lost. There&rsquo;s a calmer way that keeps every single picture.'}, {'t': 'Back photos up first &mdash; for example with Google Photos &mdash; then use its option to free up space', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly right. Back up first, then let the app clear only the copies that are already safe. Nothing precious is lost.'}, {'t': 'Ignore the warning &mdash; it&rsquo;s probably nothing', 'ok': False, 'why': 'The warning is genuine &mdash; a completely full phone can start to misbehave. But there&rsquo;s no rush: back up first, then free space safely.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128267;', 'tag': 'Lesson 5 of 7', 'title': 'Keeping your phone healthy', 'body': '<p><strong>Updates are free, and they protect you.</strong> When your phone offers a software update, it&rsquo;s mostly delivering security repairs &mdash; think of a locksmith quietly upgrading your locks. Let it install, ideally on Wi-Fi with the phone charging, and carry on. The same goes for app updates from the Play Store.</p><p><strong>Restarting cures a surprising amount.</strong> If the phone feels slow, the keyboard lags, or an app misbehaves, switch it off and on again. It sounds far too simple to work &mdash; it genuinely works, and it&rsquo;s always the first thing to try.</p><p>And some honesty about <strong>batteries</strong>: they wear gradually with age, and that&rsquo;s normal, not a fault. Modern phones manage their own charging sensibly, so charging overnight is fine. The biggest battery user is usually the screen, so slightly lower brightness means a longer day. And if an older phone barely lasts the morning, a battery replacement is often far cheaper than a new phone.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">&#128276; Notification</p>System update available. Your phone will restart during installation.</div>', 'q': 'What&rsquo;s the best response?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Ignore it &mdash; updates just slow old phones down', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A very common worry, but updates are mostly free security repairs &mdash; skipping them leaves the door unlocked. They&rsquo;re well worth having.'}, {'t': 'Let it install, ideally on Wi-Fi with the phone charging', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Perfect. Updates are free and protective, and Wi-Fi plus a charger is the comfortable way to take them. Then carry on as normal.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128737;&#65039;', 'tag': 'Lesson 6 of 7', 'title': 'Phone scams and staying safe', 'body': '<p><strong>Calls from unknown numbers:</strong> you never have to answer. Let it ring &mdash; anyone genuine will leave a voicemail or try again, while scammers usually just move on. If you do answer and the caller pressures you, asks about your bank, or claims your phone or computer &ldquo;has a problem&rdquo;, simply hang up. Hanging up is never rude when someone is trying to trick you. And you can forward scam <em>texts</em> to <strong>7726</strong> &mdash; a free service that reports them straight to your mobile network.</p><p><strong>&ldquo;Your phone has a virus!&rdquo; pop-ups:</strong> while browsing the web you may meet an alarming, flashing warning urging you to tap a button or install a cleaner app. It&rsquo;s an advert dressed up as an emergency &mdash; a web page cannot actually see viruses on your phone. <strong>Don&rsquo;t tap anything on it.</strong> Close that page &mdash; or close the whole browser app and press Home &mdash; and the &ldquo;emergency&rdquo; simply vanishes.</p><p>Finally, set a <strong>screen lock</strong> &mdash; a PIN, pattern or fingerprint. If your phone is ever lost, it means whoever picks it up cannot get into your messages, photos or banking. You&rsquo;ll find it in Settings under something like <strong>Security</strong> (the wording varies by maker). A fingerprint, on phones that offer one, is both the easiest to use and the hardest to guess.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">&#9888;&#65039; Pop-up while browsing</p>WARNING! Your Android is infected with 5 viruses! Tap CLEAN NOW immediately, before your photos are deleted!</div>', 'q': 'What do you do?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Tap CLEAN NOW &mdash; it says my photos are at risk', 'ok': False, 'why': 'That panic is the whole trick &mdash; a web page can&rsquo;t see viruses on your phone. Tapping is the only thing that could cause trouble, so leave it well alone.'}, {'t': 'Don&rsquo;t tap anything &mdash; close the page (or the whole browser) and carry on', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly. It&rsquo;s a fake advert dressed as an emergency. Close it, press Home, and it&rsquo;s gone &mdash; your phone was never infected.'}, {'t': 'Install the cleaner app it recommends', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Anything a pop-up pushes you to install is best avoided. If you ever want a security app, go to the Play Store yourself and pick a well-reviewed one.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#129309;', 'tag': 'Lesson 7 of 7', 'title': 'When to ask for help (and why it&rsquo;s smart)', 'body': '<p>Everyone gets stuck &mdash; everyone. The people who become confident with their phones aren&rsquo;t the ones who never hit a snag; they&rsquo;re the ones who ask. Asking is a skill, not a failing.</p><ul><li><strong>Write down what happened</strong> &mdash; what you tapped, what the screen said. Even a rough note makes helping you ten times easier</li><li><strong>Keep a little list of questions</strong> for when you next see family, a friend, or us</li><li><strong>Never let anyone rush you</strong> &mdash; and never hand over your phone or its PIN to a stranger who rings up claiming they&rsquo;ll fix it. Genuine helpers don&rsquo;t call out of the blue</li></ul><p>And remember, you have us. We&rsquo;re 365 Techies, a family firm in Bournemouth since 1995. Friendly help is one phone call away on <strong>01202 775566</strong>, and we run relaxed in-person lessons at the <strong>Kinson Community Centre</strong> in Bournemouth &mdash; bring your own phone and ask anything. There is no such thing as a silly question. We mean that.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'You&rsquo;ve tried restarting, you&rsquo;re still stuck, and you&rsquo;re starting to feel a bit silly. What now?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Give up &mdash; phones clearly aren&rsquo;t for me', 'ok': False, 'why': 'They absolutely are for you &mdash; you&rsquo;ve just met a snag, and everyone does. One question to the right person usually clears it in minutes.'}, {'t': 'Note down what happened and ask someone &mdash; family, a friend, or give us a ring', 'ok': True, 'why': 'That&rsquo;s exactly it. Asking is what confident phone users do &mdash; and we&rsquo;re always at the end of the phone on 01202 775566.'}]}}], 'doneBlurb': '<p>Congratulations &mdash; you&rsquo;ve finished the course, and your phone is officially tamed. If you keep just one habit, keep this one: <strong>explore without fear</strong>. You can&rsquo;t easily break anything, and the Home button (or swipe) will always bring you safely back.</p><p>And if you&rsquo;re ever stuck, friendly help is one phone call away on <strong>01202 775566</strong> &mdash; or join our relaxed in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth.</p>', 'certCourseName': 'Your Android Phone, Tamed', 'certSubline': 'Home screen confidence, essential settings, safe apps, photos, updates and phone scam awareness', 'checklist': ['Explore freely: the Home button or swipe always rescues you', 'Make the text bigger in Settings (usually under Display)', 'Install apps only from the Google Play Store', 'Check reviews and permissions before installing an app', 'Back up photos before freeing up space', 'Let free software updates install', 'Restart the phone first when anything misbehaves', 'Never tap virus warning pop-ups: close the page instead', 'Set a screen lock: a PIN or a fingerprint', 'When stuck, ask: 365 Techies is on 01202 775566'], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Is the course really free?', 'a': '<p>Completely free &mdash; no catch, and nothing to sign up for. We&rsquo;re a family IT firm in Bournemouth, and courses like this are our way of helping local people feel confident with technology. If you ever want more help, you know where we are.</p>'}, {'q': 'Which phones does it cover?', 'a': '<p>Any Android phone &mdash; Samsung, Google, Motorola and others. Menus and wording differ a little between makers, so we teach by what buttons <em>do</em> rather than exact taps. If you have an iPhone, Apple offers similar features, but this course is written for Android.</p>'}, {'q': 'Could I break my phone by practising?', 'a': '<p>It&rsquo;s very hard to break anything by tapping and exploring &mdash; that&rsquo;s lesson one! The Home button (or swipe) always takes you back to the start, and nothing in this course asks you to do anything risky.</p>'}, {'q': 'Do I get a certificate?', 'a': '<p>Yes &mdash; when you finish, type your first name and print your 365 Techies certificate of completion. It&rsquo;s our own friendly certificate &mdash; not an accredited or formal qualification &mdash; and plenty of people pop it on the fridge.</p>'}, {'q': 'Do I have to finish it in one sitting?', 'a': '<p>Not at all &mdash; go entirely at your own pace. Your progress is saved on your device, so you can close the page and pick up exactly where you left off, cup of tea in hand, another day.</p>'}, {'q': 'What if I get stuck or would rather learn with a real person?', 'a': '<p>Ring us on 01202 775566 &mdash; friendly help is one phone call away. We also run relaxed in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth, where you can bring your own phone and ask anything at all.</p>'}], 'crumbName': 'Your Android Phone, Tamed'},
{'slug': 'online-banking-safely-course', 'title': 'Free Online Banking Safety Course for Over-60s | 365 Techies', 'metaDesc': 'A free, gentle online banking course for older beginners. Learn the golden rules that keep your money safe, at your own pace, with a certificate at the end.', 'h1': 'Online banking, <em class="grad grad--green">without the worry</em>', 'heroLede': 'Many people avoid online banking because of scare stories &mdash; this free course gives you the honest picture, and the few golden rules that genuinely keep your money safe. Work through seven short lessons at your own pace, then print your certificate with your name on it. No jargon, no rush, no catch.', 'icon': '&#127974;', 'courseTitle': 'Online Banking, Safely', 'tagline': 'Free &middot; 7 short lessons &middot; about 40 minutes', 'introBody': '<p>Welcome &mdash; and if you feel a little nervous about this one, you are in the best possible company. Online banking is the subject people most often tell us they have been avoiding, usually because of frightening stories in the news. This course was written to give you the honest picture: what online banking actually is, why your money is protected, and the small set of rules that keep it that way.</p><p>There&rsquo;s no test, no timer, and nothing you can break. Take the lessons in order or dip in and out &mdash; and if anything leaves you stuck, we&rsquo;re one phone call away on 01202 775566.</p>', 'modules': [{'ico': '&#127974;', 'tag': 'Lesson 1 of 7', 'title': 'Why everyone keeps mentioning online banking', 'body': '<p>If your local branch has closed, you are not alone &mdash; a great many bank branches have shut across the UK in recent years, and banks now do most of their day-to-day work through the phone in your pocket or the computer on your desk. That can feel like a door closing. This course is about the door that opened instead.</p><p>Online banking simply means looking at your own bank account on a screen instead of over a counter. You can check your balance, see what has gone in and out, and pay a bill &mdash; from your armchair, at any hour, with no bus ride and no queue.</p><p>And your money is not floating loose on the internet. UK banks are strictly regulated, an official UK compensation scheme stands behind the money in your account (up to a set limit) in the unlikely event a bank itself ever failed, and if money ever left your account without your permission, your bank would normally have to put it back. Looking at your account on a screen does not make the money in it any less yours &mdash; or any less safe.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'A friend says: &ldquo;I&rsquo;d never look at my bank account online &mdash; one wrong tap and my money could vanish.&rdquo; What&rsquo;s the truth?', 'opts': [{'t': 'She&rsquo;s right &mdash; it&rsquo;s safer to avoid online banking completely', 'ok': False, 'why': 'It&rsquo;s a very understandable worry &mdash; but avoiding it doesn&rsquo;t actually make you safer. Scams nearly always arrive by phone call or text, not because someone looked at their balance. In fact, being able to check your account easily helps you spot problems sooner.'}, {'t': 'Simply looking at your account can&rsquo;t move your money anywhere &mdash; and UK accounts are protected by law', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly right. Checking a balance or reading a statement moves nothing at all. Money only moves when you deliberately set up a payment and confirm it &mdash; and later lessons show you how to do that safely too.'}, {'t': 'It&rsquo;s safe, but only if you buy a brand-new computer first', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Good news &mdash; no new kit needed. Any reasonably up-to-date phone, tablet or computer is fine. What keeps you safe isn&rsquo;t the age of the machine; it&rsquo;s the handful of golden rules coming up in Lesson 4.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128241;', 'tag': 'Lesson 2 of 7', 'title': 'The app or the website?', 'body': '<p>There are two front doors to online banking: the bank&rsquo;s <strong>app</strong> (a button on your phone or tablet) and the bank&rsquo;s <strong>website</strong> (typed into a computer). Both are genuinely safe when used properly &mdash; but for most people, the app is the easier and safer way in.</p><p>Why? An app downloaded from the official store &mdash; the <strong>Google Play Store</strong> on Android phones &mdash; talks directly to your bank. There is no web address to mistype and no fake lookalike page to stumble onto. Most banking apps unlock with your fingerprint, your face or a short PIN, so there is less typing to get wrong. (Apple devices have their own equivalent app store.)</p><p>If you prefer the website, that&rsquo;s fine too: type the bank&rsquo;s address in yourself, or use a bookmark you saved earlier. The one rule that never changes: <strong>never log in through a link that arrived in a text or an email</strong> &mdash; that is exactly how fake sites catch people out.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">Text message received</p><p>&ldquo;Your banking app is out of date and will stop working. Download the new secure version here: bank-secure-update.com&rdquo;</p></div>', 'q': 'You want your bank&rsquo;s app on your Android phone. Where should it come from?', 'opts': [{'t': 'The link in the text &mdash; it sounds urgent and official', 'ok': False, 'why': 'This is exactly the trap, and it fools plenty of people &mdash; real banks don&rsquo;t send app downloads by text. No harm in having wondered, though: the fact that you paused to think is the whole skill.'}, {'t': 'The Google Play Store, searching for the bank by name', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Spot on. On Android phones, the Play Store is the official, checked source &mdash; and apps you already have normally keep themselves up to date through it, so a text like this is never genuine. Forward it free to 7726 and delete it.'}, {'t': 'Any website that offers the app as a free download', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A reasonable guess, but apps from random websites can be fakes dressed up to look real. Stick to the official store &mdash; on Android, that&rsquo;s the Google Play Store &mdash; and you sidestep the problem entirely.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#127793;', 'tag': 'Lesson 3 of 7', 'title': 'Setting up, gently', 'body': '<p>Here is the most reassuring thing we tell people at our lessons in Kinson: <strong>you cannot break your bank account by looking at it.</strong> Tapping around, opening menus, reading screens &mdash; none of it moves a penny. Money only ever moves when you deliberately set up a payment and confirm it.</p><p>You don&rsquo;t have to set things up alone, either. Your bank will help you register &mdash; most offer help over the phone, or in a branch if you can reach one. They do this every day, for people of every age, and they are patient about it. You&rsquo;ll usually need your account details and your bank card handy, and the bank will guide you through choosing a password and getting the app working.</p><p>Then go at your own pace. Set it up one day, just have a look around the next, check your balance the day after. There are no prizes for rushing &mdash; and no penalties at all for taking your time.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'You&rsquo;ve just opened your new banking app for the first time, and you&rsquo;re nervous about pressing the wrong thing. Which of these is true?', 'opts': [{'t': 'One stray tap could send your money somewhere by accident', 'ok': False, 'why': 'This is the single most common fear we hear &mdash; and happily, it&rsquo;s wrong. A payment needs several deliberate steps and a final confirmation before anything moves. Browsing, tapping and reading are completely safe.'}, {'t': 'Looking around can&rsquo;t move your money &mdash; payments always need deliberate steps and a confirmation', 'ok': True, 'why': 'That&rsquo;s it exactly. Explore as freely as you like &mdash; it&rsquo;s the best way to get comfortable. The app is a window onto your account, not a trapdoor under it.'}, {'t': 'You should only open the app when the bank telephones and tells you to', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Gently, no &mdash; in fact it&rsquo;s closer to the other way round. Your app is yours to open whenever you please. A caller instructing you to open it and do things is a warning sign we&rsquo;ll cover in the next lesson.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128274;', 'tag': 'Lesson 4 of 7', 'title': 'The golden rules your bank lives by', 'body': '<p>These few rules are worth more than everything else in this course put together. Your bank will <strong>never</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Ask you to move money to a &ldquo;safe account&rdquo;.</strong> Safe accounts do not exist. Anyone telling you your money is at risk and must be moved is a criminal &mdash; every single time, no exceptions.</li><li><strong>Ask for your full PIN or full password.</strong> The real bank may ask for certain characters, but never the whole thing.</li><li><strong>Mind you hanging up.</strong> A genuine bank employee is entirely happy for you to put the phone down and call back on a number you trust. Only a scammer objects to that.</li></ul><p>One more rule, about <strong>one-time codes</strong> &mdash; the short codes (often six digits) your bank sends by text or shows in its app when you log in or pay. They exist for you to <em>type into a screen</em>, never to read out loud. No bank employee, no police officer, nobody genuine will ever ask you to say one down the phone. A voice asking for a code is a scammer &mdash; every single time.</p><p>Finally, remember the number <strong>159</strong>. If you ever get a call claiming to be your bank, hang up, wait a moment for the line to clear, then dial 159 &mdash; it puts you safely through to your own bank so you can check. At the time of writing, most major UK banks are connected to it.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">Phone call, 2:15pm</p><p>&ldquo;This is the fraud team at your bank. Criminals have accessed your account this afternoon. We need you to move your money to a safe account right now, while there&rsquo;s still time.&rdquo;</p></div>', 'q': 'What do you do?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Do as they say quickly &mdash; the fraud team knows best', 'ok': False, 'why': 'The urgency is the giveaway, and it&rsquo;s designed to be convincing &mdash; this exact call has fooled very clever people. But the rule has no exceptions: banks never, ever ask you to move money to a safe account. Only criminals do.'}, {'t': 'Hang up, wait a moment, then dial 159 to reach your real bank', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Perfect. &ldquo;Safe accounts&rdquo; do not exist, so the call cannot be genuine &mdash; and hanging up on a real bank employee never causes offence. Dialling 159 yourself means you know exactly who you&rsquo;re talking to.'}, {'t': 'Ask the caller to prove who they are by reading out some of your details', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A fair instinct &mdash; but scammers often already know your name and some details, which is precisely what makes them sound real. The safe move is simpler and stronger: hang up and dial 159 yourself.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#9989;', 'tag': 'Lesson 5 of 7', 'title': 'Paying someone: the name check', 'body': '<p>When you set up a payment to someone new, most UK banks now run a helpful check called <strong>Confirmation of Payee</strong>. You type in the sort code, the account number and the name of the person or company &mdash; and the bank tells you whether that name matches the real account before any money moves.</p><p>Treat the answer like a traffic light. A full match means carry on. A partial match or <strong>no match means stop</strong> &mdash; don&rsquo;t send a penny until you&rsquo;ve spoken to the person on a phone number you already know and checked the details together.</p><p>Paying someone new for the first time deserves a triple-check anyway: read the account number back digit by digit, and for a larger amount, many people send &pound;1 first and confirm it arrived before sending the rest. Sensible, not paranoid.</p><p>Worth knowing in general terms: paying by <strong>card</strong> comes with strong protections if something goes wrong, and credit cards add extra legal protection on larger purchases. A <strong>bank transfer</strong> is more like handing over cash &mdash; much harder to claw back &mdash; so transfers deserve the most care of all, especially to someone new.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">Setting up a payment</p><p>You&rsquo;re paying a gardener &pound;180 for the first time. You type in his details and your bank replies: &ldquo;The name on this account does not match the name you entered.&rdquo;</p></div>', 'q': 'What&rsquo;s the wise move?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Send it anyway &mdash; banks get these checks wrong all the time', 'ok': False, 'why': 'The check is there precisely for moments like this, and it&rsquo;s usually right. A no-match doesn&rsquo;t mean disaster &mdash; but it does mean pause. One quick phone call costs nothing; a payment to the wrong account is hard to undo.'}, {'t': 'Stop, and ring the gardener on the number you already have to re-check the details', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly right. Often it&rsquo;s just a typo or a business trading under a different name &mdash; but sometimes it&rsquo;s a fraudster who has slipped you fake details. Either way, one phone call sorts it before any money moves.'}, {'t': 'Send &pound;1 first as a test, then send the rest', 'ok': False, 'why': 'The &pound;1 test is a lovely habit when the name matches and you just want belt and braces &mdash; but when the name doesn&rsquo;t match, even &pound;1 shouldn&rsquo;t go anywhere yet. Check with the gardener first, then test away.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#127917;', 'tag': 'Lesson 6 of 7', 'title': 'The bank that isn&rsquo;t your bank', 'body': '<p>Bank-impersonation scams almost always arrive one of two ways: an <strong>urgent phone call</strong> (&ldquo;your account has been compromised&rdquo;) or an <strong>alarming text</strong> (&ldquo;a payment has been attempted &mdash; click here to verify&rdquo;). The costume changes; the script never does. First frighten you, then rush you.</p><p>Two tricks make these convincing. Scammers can make a call or text <em>appear</em> to come from your bank&rsquo;s real number &mdash; it&rsquo;s called spoofing, and it means the number on your screen proves nothing, even if the text lands in the same thread as genuine messages. And they may know your name or other details about you. Neither means it&rsquo;s really the bank.</p><p>Happily, the defence never changes either. Suspicious call? Hang up, wait, dial <strong>159</strong>. Suspicious text? Don&rsquo;t tap the link &mdash; forward it free to <strong>7726</strong>, then delete it. Suspicious email? Send it to <strong>report@phishing.gov.uk</strong>. And if you want the truth about your account, open your banking app yourself or call the number on the back of your card. The Take Five campaign sums it up nicely: stop, challenge, protect.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">Text message, Saturday morning</p><p>&ldquo;BANK ALERT: A payment of &pound;900 was attempted from your account. If this was NOT you, verify your details immediately: [link]&rdquo; &mdash; and it has appeared in the same thread as real texts from your bank.</p></div>', 'q': 'What do you do?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Tap the link straight away &mdash; &pound;900 is a lot of money', 'ok': False, 'why': 'That jolt of alarm is exactly what the message is engineered to produce &mdash; it happens to almost everyone. But real banks don&rsquo;t ask you to &ldquo;verify&rdquo; through a text link. Your own app or the number on your card will tell you the truth safely.'}, {'t': 'Don&rsquo;t tap it. Check your account through your own banking app, and forward the text free to 7726', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Perfect on every count. Because numbers can be spoofed, even a text sitting among genuine ones proves nothing &mdash; but your own app tells you the truth in ten seconds. Forwarding to 7726 helps get the scam number shut down too.'}, {'t': 'Reply STOP so the texts stop coming', 'ok': False, 'why': 'With genuine companies STOP works a treat &mdash; but replying to a scam text just tells the criminals your number is live, so more may follow. Forward it to 7726, delete it, and check your account through your own app.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#129534;', 'tag': 'Lesson 7 of 7', 'title': 'The five-minute habit that keeps you safe', 'body': '<p>Here is online banking&rsquo;s best-kept secret: it can make you <em>safer</em> than paper statements ever did, because you can look at your account any day you like &mdash; not once a month when the envelope arrives.</p><p>Make it a little routine. Once a week or so, open the app with a cup of tea and glance down the list of payments, asking one simple question: <em>do I recognise all of these?</em> Don&rsquo;t panic over an unfamiliar shop name &mdash; companies sometimes appear under a different trading name on statements. But anything you truly can&rsquo;t place is worth a phone call.</p><p>If something really is wrong, speed is your friend. Tell your bank straight away &mdash; dial 159 or call the number on the back of your card &mdash; because banks can often stop or recover a fraudulent payment, and when you report promptly and the fraud wasn&rsquo;t your fault, you would normally be refunded. Fraud can also be reported to Action Fraud on 0300 123 2040.</p><p>And keep your phone and its apps updated &mdash; updates are free security repairs, quietly fixing the holes scammers hope to use.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">Your statement, Tuesday</p><p>&pound;34.99 to &ldquo;GRN*DIGITALSVCS&rdquo; &mdash; and you can&rsquo;t remember buying anything like that.</p></div>', 'q': 'What&rsquo;s the best move?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Wait a few weeks and see whether it happens again', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Waiting feels sensible, but with fraud the clock matters &mdash; the sooner your bank knows, the more it can do, and acting quickly is what keeps you protected. One short call today beats a bigger worry next month.'}, {'t': 'Try to place the name, and if you still can&rsquo;t, call your bank today', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Just right. Quite often it turns out to be a familiar shop under an unfamiliar trading name &mdash; but if it is fraud, prompt reporting means the bank can act fast, and you&rsquo;re normally refunded when it wasn&rsquo;t your fault.'}, {'t': 'Post it on Facebook and ask if anyone recognises the company', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Friends mean well, but your bank can answer in two minutes flat &mdash; and it&rsquo;s best not to share anything from your statement publicly. Save the post; make the call.'}]}}], 'doneBlurb': '<p>Well done &mdash; you&rsquo;ve finished the whole course, and you now know the rules that genuinely keep online banking safe. That honestly puts you ahead of most people of any age.</p><p>If you keep just one habit, keep this one: <strong>a real bank will never rush you.</strong> Anyone who hurries, frightens or pressures you on the phone is not your bank &mdash; so hang up, take a breath, and dial 159. And if you&rsquo;d ever like a friendly human alongside you, call us on 01202 775566, or come and see us at our in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth.</p>', 'certCourseName': 'Online Banking, Safely', 'certSubline': 'For learning the golden rules of safe online banking &mdash; official apps, one-time codes, payment name checks, 159 and spotting bank-impersonation scams', 'checklist': ['Get your banking app only from the Google Play Store on Android', 'Never log in through a link sent by text or email', 'Your bank will NEVER ask you to move money to a safe account', 'One-time codes are for typing into screens, never for reading aloud', 'Never give anyone your full PIN or full password', 'Suspicious call from the bank? Hang up, wait, then dial 159', 'Forward scam texts free to 7726, then delete them', 'Paying someone new? Check the name matches and triple-check the details', 'Glance through your payments each week and ring the bank about anything odd', 'Stuck or worried? Call 365 Techies on 01202 775566'], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Is online banking really safe for someone my age?', 'a': '<p>Yes &mdash; genuinely. UK banks are strictly regulated, an official compensation scheme stands behind the money in your account (up to a set limit), and payments you never authorised would normally have to be refunded. The real risk isn&rsquo;t the technology; it&rsquo;s scammers on the phone pretending to be your bank &mdash; and spotting them is exactly what this course teaches.</p>'}, {'q': 'Do I need a computer, or will my phone do?', 'a': '<p>A smartphone or tablet is all you need &mdash; for most people, the bank&rsquo;s own app is the easiest and safest way in. Everything in this course works on Android phones and Windows computers; Apple devices have their own equivalents.</p>'}, {'q': 'What happens if I press the wrong button?', 'a': '<p>Nothing dramatic, we promise. Simply looking around your account can&rsquo;t move any money &mdash; a payment always needs several deliberate steps and a final confirmation. You can explore to your heart&rsquo;s content.</p>'}, {'q': 'Is this course really free? What&rsquo;s the catch?', 'a': '<p>Completely free &mdash; no card details, no trial, no catch. We&rsquo;re a family IT firm in Bournemouth, going since 1995, and we&rsquo;d simply rather local people were safe and confident. If you ever want hands-on help, we hope you&rsquo;ll think of us.</p>'}, {'q': 'Do I get a certificate, and is it a qualification?', 'a': '<p>When you finish, you can type in your first name and print a 365 Techies certificate of completion. It&rsquo;s a lovely thing to keep on the fridge, though not a formal qualification &mdash; the real prize is the confidence.</p>'}, {'q': 'What if I get stuck partway through?', 'a': '<p>Help is one phone call away &mdash; ring us on 01202 775566 and a friendly human will talk it through with you. And if you&rsquo;d rather learn face to face, we run in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth.</p>'}], 'crumbName': 'Online Banking, Safely'},
{'slug': 'video-calling-course-for-beginners', 'title': 'Free Video Calling Course for Beginners | 365 Techies', 'metaDesc': 'A free, gentle video-calling course for older beginners in the UK. Learn WhatsApp and Zoom calls step by step, fix common hiccups and print your certificate.', 'h1': 'See their faces &mdash; <em class="grad grad--green">video calling</em> made simple', 'heroLede': 'Grandchildren growing up fast, or friends on the other side of the world? This free course gently shows you how to see their faces on a video call &mdash; step by step, at your own pace, and with a printable certificate waiting at the end.', 'icon': '&#128249;', 'courseTitle': 'See Their Faces: Video Calling', 'tagline': 'Free · 6 short lessons · about 30 minutes', 'introBody': '<p>There&rsquo;s something rather wonderful about seeing a face light up when a call connects &mdash; a grandchild showing off a lost tooth, an old friend waving from their kitchen in Spain. That&rsquo;s what this little course is for.</p><p>You don&rsquo;t need any experience, and you almost certainly don&rsquo;t need to buy anything. Six short lessons, taken at whatever pace suits you &mdash; and by the end, the people you love will be one green button away.</p>', 'modules': [{'ico': '&#128241;', 'tag': 'Lesson 1 of 6', 'title': 'You already have everything you need', 'body': '<p>Here&rsquo;s some good news to start with: if you own a smartphone, a tablet or a laptop made in the last ten years or so, it almost certainly has a camera and microphone built in. You very likely already own everything you need.</p><p>The other ingredient is an internet connection. And here&rsquo;s the honest bit about cost:</p><ul><li><strong>Over your home Wi-Fi:</strong> video calls cost nothing extra, however long you natter.</li><li><strong>Over mobile data</strong> (out and about, away from Wi-Fi): video uses up your monthly data allowance quite quickly, so the long catch-ups are best saved for home.</li></ul><p>That&rsquo;s genuinely it. No webcam to buy, no special equipment &mdash; just the device you already have, and your Wi-Fi.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'Your daughter suggests a Sunday video call. You have a smartphone and home Wi-Fi. What do you need to buy first?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Nothing at all &mdash; my phone and Wi-Fi are already everything I need', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly right. The camera and microphone are built in, and over home Wi-Fi the call costs nothing extra. You&rsquo;re ready to go.'}, {'t': 'A separate webcam and a proper headset', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Good instinct to come prepared &mdash; but there&rsquo;s no need. Your phone&rsquo;s built-in camera and microphone were made for exactly this. Save your money for biscuits.'}, {'t': 'A newer, more expensive phone', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Happily not! Almost any smartphone from the last decade handles video calls beautifully. The one you already have is very likely just fine.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128172;', 'tag': 'Lesson 2 of 6', 'title': 'Your first WhatsApp video call', 'body': '<p>If your family already use WhatsApp for messages, video calling is the easiest possible next step &mdash; it lives inside the same app you already have.</p><p>Open your chat with the person, then look for the <strong>little video camera icon</strong> near the top of the screen. Its exact spot varies a little by phone and app version, but the icon is always a small camera. Tap it, and it rings them &mdash; just like a phone call, but with faces.</p><p>When someone video-calls <em>you</em>, your phone rings and shows who&rsquo;s calling. Tap or swipe the <strong>green accept button</strong>, exactly as you&rsquo;d answer any call. If the first thing you see is your own startled face, don&rsquo;t worry &mdash; that&rsquo;s just the camera waking up.</p><p>Best of all: WhatsApp calls travel over the internet, so on Wi-Fi they&rsquo;re <strong>free &mdash; even to family in Australia or Canada</strong>, however long you talk. The calls are also end-to-end encrypted &mdash; which simply means nobody in between, not even WhatsApp itself, can watch or listen in.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">&#128172; WhatsApp message from your son in Sydney</p>Fancy a video call on Sunday? The kids want to show you the new puppy! We&rsquo;d be on for at least an hour though&hellip;</div>', 'q': 'An hour-long video call to Australia &mdash; what will it cost you on your home Wi-Fi?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Nothing extra at all, however long we chat', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Spot on. WhatsApp calls travel over the internet, so on Wi-Fi an hour to Australia costs the same as a minute to next door: nothing extra. Enjoy the puppy!'}, {'t': 'International rates, charged by the minute like a phone call', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A very sensible worry &mdash; that&rsquo;s exactly how phone calls used to work! But WhatsApp calls go over the internet instead, so on Wi-Fi they&rsquo;re free wherever in the world your family happen to be.'}, {'t': 'It&rsquo;s free, but only for the first ten minutes', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Happily there&rsquo;s no time limit &mdash; over Wi-Fi you can chat all afternoon at no extra cost. The puppy will get bored before your Wi-Fi does.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128101;', 'tag': 'Lesson 3 of 6', 'title': 'Zoom &mdash; joining the whole family at once', 'body': '<p>WhatsApp is lovely for one or two faces. For a whole family quiz, a church group or a club committee, many people use <strong>Zoom</strong>, which comfortably fits lots of people on screen at once.</p><p>Here&rsquo;s the part that surprises people: <strong>to join someone else&rsquo;s Zoom call, you don&rsquo;t need an account</strong>. Whoever organises the call sends you a link &mdash; by email or WhatsApp &mdash; and the link does all the work. Press it at the right time and follow the simple prompts. The very first time, your device may ask to install the free Zoom app, and it may ask permission to use your camera and microphone &mdash; both are perfectly normal, so say yes, and you&rsquo;re in.</p><p>Once you&rsquo;re in the call, tap or click the screen and the controls appear, usually along one edge: a <strong>microphone button</strong> for your voice and a <strong>camera button</strong> for your picture. Each switches on and off when pressed &mdash; a line through the icon means it&rsquo;s off.</p><p>Which brings us to the most famous sentence in video calling: <em>&ldquo;You&rsquo;re on mute!&rdquo;</em> If everyone&rsquo;s smiling but nobody can hear you, glance at the microphone button. It happens to prime ministers and grandparents alike &mdash; one tap fixes it.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">&#9993;&#65039; Email from your grandson</p>Hi Gran! Family quiz on Zoom, Saturday 7pm &mdash; just click this link to join. No need to set anything up!</div>', 'q': 'You&rsquo;ve never used Zoom and don&rsquo;t have an account. What do you do?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Press the link just before 7pm and follow the prompts &mdash; no account needed', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Exactly. The link does all the work &mdash; joining someone else&rsquo;s Zoom call needs no account and costs nothing. See you at the quiz!'}, {'t': 'Email back to say you&rsquo;ll need to set up a paid Zoom account first', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Kind of you to want to be ready &mdash; but there&rsquo;s no need. Joining a call someone else has organised is free and needs no account at all. The link really is all it takes.'}, {'t': 'Ask him to phone you instead &mdash; Zoom is only for offices', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Zoom did become famous through office meetings, but families use it every day for quizzes and birthdays. And since his link needs no account, you&rsquo;re only one press away from joining in.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128161;', 'tag': 'Lesson 4 of 6', 'title': 'Looking and sounding your best', 'body': '<p>Three little tricks make a world of difference, and none of them costs a penny.</p><ul><li><strong>Put the light in front of you, not behind.</strong> Sit facing a window or a lamp. If the light is behind you, the camera sees only a mysterious silhouette &mdash; dramatic, but not what the grandchildren rang for.</li><li><strong>Prop the device at eye height.</strong> A phone flat on the table gives everyone a fine view up your nose. Lean it against a pile of books or a shelf so the camera looks straight at you &mdash; instantly more natural, and your arm gets a rest too.</li><li><strong>Speak normally.</strong> The microphone is designed to pick up an ordinary speaking voice from an arm&rsquo;s length away. There&rsquo;s no need to lean in and shout &mdash; just chat as you always do and you&rsquo;ll come through loud and clear.</li></ul><p>Do those three things and you&rsquo;ll look and sound better than half the people on television.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '', 'q': 'You join a call and the family say you&rsquo;re just a dark shadow against the window. What&rsquo;s the fix?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Turn round so the window light falls on your face instead of coming from behind you', 'ok': True, 'why': 'That&rsquo;s the one. Light in front, not behind &mdash; turn your chair or move the device, and you&rsquo;ll go from silhouette to star in two seconds.'}, {'t': 'Accept it &mdash; the camera must be too old', 'ok': False, 'why': 'It&rsquo;s almost never the camera! Even brand-new phones show a silhouette when a bright window sits behind you. Simply facing the light fixes it &mdash; no new equipment required.'}, {'t': 'Speak louder, so at least they can hear you clearly', 'ok': False, 'why': 'They can already hear you just fine &mdash; the trouble here is light, not sound. Face the window rather than sitting in front of it and they&rsquo;ll see you beautifully.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128260;', 'tag': 'Lesson 5 of 6', 'title': 'When it goes wrong (and it does, for everyone)', 'body': '<p>First, the most reassuring fact in this whole course: <strong>when a video call misbehaves, it is almost never your fault</strong>, and you almost certainly haven&rsquo;t broken anything. It happens to everyone, regularly.</p><ul><li><strong>Frozen or blocky picture, robot-sounding voice:</strong> usually the Wi-Fi signal, not the device. Moving a little closer to your router (the box from your internet company, usually blinking away near the phone socket or the TV) often works wonders.</li><li><strong>Hearing your own voice echo back:</strong> a common quirk when two devices in the same house are on the same call. Turning the volume down a touch, muting one of the devices, or moving to another room usually settles it.</li><li><strong>A Zoom call that suddenly ends mid-conversation:</strong> Zoom&rsquo;s free version puts a time limit on group calls &mdash; around 40 minutes, at the time of writing. Nobody has broken anything: the organiser simply starts the call again, and everyone presses the same link.</li></ul><p>And when in doubt, use the fix the professionals use: <strong>leave the call and rejoin</strong>. Hang up, wait a moment, then call back &mdash; or press the Zoom link again. It cures most problems, nobody minds in the slightest, and &ldquo;sorry, I dropped out!&rdquo; is said on video calls every minute of every day.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">&#128249; Ten minutes into a lovely catch-up</p>Your niece&rsquo;s picture suddenly freezes mid-laugh, and her voice starts sounding like a robot underwater.</div>', 'q': 'What&rsquo;s the best move?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Hang up, move a little closer to the router, and call back', 'ok': True, 'why': 'The classic fix. A freeze usually means the Wi-Fi hiccupped &mdash; leaving and rejoining, ideally a bit nearer the router, cures it far more often than not.'}, {'t': 'Keep talking to the frozen picture and hope it sorts itself out', 'ok': False, 'why': 'It occasionally recovers by itself, but you could be chatting away to a frozen photograph for quite a while! Leaving and rejoining is much quicker &mdash; and your niece will still be there.'}, {'t': 'Switch everything off for the day &mdash; you must have broken something', 'ok': False, 'why': 'You haven&rsquo;t broken a thing &mdash; promise. Frozen pictures are nearly always the Wi-Fi having a moment, and rejoining the call fixes it. No need to give up on the afternoon.'}]}}, {'ico': '&#128737;&#65039;', 'tag': 'Lesson 6 of 6', 'title': 'Staying safe on camera', 'body': '<p>Video calling is one of the safest, happiest things you can do online &mdash; you can see exactly who you&rsquo;re talking to. Three sensible rules keep it that way.</p><ul><li><strong>Unexpected video call from a stranger? Decline it</strong> &mdash; just as you would a knock at the door from someone you don&rsquo;t know. Anyone genuine will message first or try again. Declining is never rude.</li><li><strong>Never do or show anything private on camera with someone you only know online.</strong> Sadly, some fraudsters spend weeks being charming precisely to coax people into intimate moments on video &mdash; then record them and demand money. It targets kind, trusting people of every age. The defence is simple: keep calls with online-only acquaintances strictly friendly, and calmly end any call that turns personal. If this ever happens to someone you know, the criminal is the only one who should feel ashamed &mdash; report it to Action Fraud on 0300 123 2040.</li><li><strong>Let video work for you.</strong> One common trick &mdash; often called the &ldquo;Hi Mum&rdquo; scam &mdash; is a message from an unknown number claiming to be family on a &ldquo;new phone&rdquo;, usually asking for money; fraudsters can even fake a relative&rsquo;s voice on a phone call these days. A video call where you can see the face settles it in seconds &mdash; and agreeing a family codeword adds an extra lock.</li></ul><p>One last note: if your family all use Apple devices, they may mention <strong>FaceTime</strong>, Apple&rsquo;s own video-calling app. Everything in this course &mdash; the lighting, the eye height, the leave-and-rejoin trick, these safety rules &mdash; applies there just the same.</p>', 'check': {'scenario': '<div class="osc-msg"><p class="osc-msg-h">&#128249; Video call request</p>Someone you met on a friendship website a few weeks ago has been wonderfully attentive. Tonight they ask for a video call &ldquo;somewhere private&rdquo;, to be kept &ldquo;just between us&rdquo;.</div>', 'q': 'What&rsquo;s the wise response?', 'opts': [{'t': 'Politely decline &mdash; keep calls with online-only friends strictly friendly, and never do anything on camera you wouldn&rsquo;t want recorded', 'ok': True, 'why': 'Wise, and kind to yourself. &ldquo;Private&rdquo; and &ldquo;just between us&rdquo; are exactly the phrases this scam leans on. A genuine friend will happily keep things ordinary &mdash; and you&rsquo;ve lost nothing by saying no.'}, {'t': 'Go along with it &mdash; video calls can&rsquo;t be recorded anyway', 'ok': False, 'why': 'Video calls sadly can be recorded, and that&rsquo;s precisely how this scam works. Nothing to feel silly about &mdash; the request itself is the warning sign, and declining is the confident move.'}, {'t': 'Agree, but ask them lots of questions first to make sure they&rsquo;re genuine', 'ok': False, 'why': 'A sensible instinct to check &mdash; but a practised fraudster answers questions smoothly. The safer rule is simpler: with someone you only know online, keep every call strictly friendly, whatever they say.'}]}}], 'doneBlurb': '<p>Well done &mdash; truly. You&rsquo;ve gone from &ldquo;where&rsquo;s the camera?&rdquo; to knowing WhatsApp calls, Zoom links, the lighting tricks, the quick fixes and the safety rules that keep it all happy. The faces you love are now one green button away.</p><p>If you keep just one habit from this course, make it this: <strong>when a call misbehaves, don&rsquo;t panic &mdash; leave and rejoin.</strong> And if you&rsquo;re ever stuck, friendly local help is one phone call away on 01202 775566, or come and practise with us in person at our lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth.</p>', 'certCourseName': 'See Their Faces: Video Calling', 'certSubline': 'WhatsApp and Zoom video calls, looking your best on camera, quick fixes and video-call safety', 'checklist': ['Use your home Wi-Fi for video calls - they cost nothing extra', 'WhatsApp: open the chat, then tap the small video camera icon', 'Answer a video call with the green button, just like any phone call', 'Zoom: press the link you were sent - no account needed to join', 'Nobody can hear you? Check the microphone button is not on mute', 'Face the light - never sit with a bright window behind you', 'Prop the device at eye height - a pile of books works well', 'Frozen picture or robot voice? Leave the call and rejoin', 'Decline unexpected video calls from strangers', 'Never do anything private on camera - video calls can be recorded'], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Do video calls really cost nothing?', 'a': '<p>Over your home Wi-Fi, yes &mdash; video calls cost nothing extra, wherever in the world the other person is. WhatsApp calls have no time limit at all, and while Zoom&rsquo;s free version does limit group calls (around 40 minutes at the time of writing), restarting costs nothing either &mdash; you just press the link again. The one thing to watch is mobile data: away from Wi-Fi, video eats into your monthly allowance quickly, so save the long catch-ups for home.</p>'}, {'q': 'Do I need to buy a webcam or any special equipment?', 'a': '<p>Almost certainly not. Any reasonably recent smartphone, tablet or laptop has a camera and microphone built in, and they were made for exactly this. Start with what you already own &mdash; very few people ever need anything more.</p>'}, {'q': 'Do I need a Zoom account to join the family quiz?', 'a': '<p>No. Joining a call that someone else has organised needs no account and costs nothing &mdash; the link they send you does all the work. You&rsquo;d only ever need an account if you wanted to organise calls yourself.</p>'}, {'q': 'My family all have iPhones and use FaceTime. Does this course still help?', 'a': '<p>Yes. FaceTime is Apple&rsquo;s video-calling app and works a little differently, but everything here about lighting, eye height, muting, fixing frozen calls and staying safe applies just the same. WhatsApp also works on both Android phones and iPhones, so it makes a lovely meeting point for mixed families.</p>'}, {'q': 'Is the course really free, and do I get a certificate?', 'a': '<p>Completely free &mdash; no account, no card details, nothing to install. At the end you can print a 365 Techies certificate of completion with your name on it. It&rsquo;s our way of saying well done rather than a formal qualification, but it looks rather smart on the fridge.</p>'}, {'q': 'What if I get stuck partway through?', 'a': '<p>Then you&rsquo;re exactly the person we made this for. Ring us on 01202 775566 and a friendly local person will help &mdash; or come along to our in-person lessons at the Kinson Community Centre in Bournemouth and we&rsquo;ll practise a video call together.</p>'}], 'crumbName': 'See Their Faces: Video Calling'},
]
